Based on your career interests in Industrial, Aerospace, and Electrical Engineering, and your preferences for Maths, Physics, and logical thinking, here are three highly-regarded universities in the UK and EU, along with a relevant Bachelor's program at each:

University of Cambridge (UK)

+ Program: Engineering (BA leading to MEng)[1]

Why it fits: Cambridge offers a world-renowned general engineering program for the first two years, covering fundamentals across disciplines like mechanical, electrical, and materials engineering. In the third and fourth years, you can specialize in areas directly relevant to your interests, such as Aerospace and Aerothermal Engineering, Electrical and Electronic Engineering, Electrical and Information Sciences, or Manufacturing Engineering (which is closely related to Industrial Engineering). This structure allows you to build a strong foundation before choosing a specific path.[2] The course integrates theoretical learning with practical application and leads to a Master of Engineering (MEng) degree after four years.

Delft University of Technology (TU Delft) (Netherlands - EU)

+ Program: BSc Aerospace Engineering (taught in English)[3]

Why it fits: TU Delft is consistently ranked among the world's best universities for aerospace engineering. This three-year Bachelor of Science program is taught entirely in English and covers all core aerospace disciplines, including aerodynamics, materials, propulsion, and orbital mechanics. The university boasts state-of-the-art facilities like wind tunnels, flight simulators, and labs for satellite development.[4] This program directly aligns with your interest in Aerospace Engineering and is situated in a leading European technical university.

Imperial College London (UK)

+ Program: MEng Electrical and Electronic Engineering[5][6][7][8][9][10]

Why it fits: Imperial is another top-tier global university, particularly strong in engineering fields. This four-year integrated Master's program focuses on Electrical and Electronic Engineering from the start, covering areas like analogue and digital electronics, power systems, communications, and control.[8] It's led by research and emphasizes practical skills alongside theory, preparing graduates for innovation in the field.[8][9] You can choose pathways later in the course, potentially including business modules or a year abroad.[9] This aligns well with your interest in Electrical Engineering and leverages your strengths in Maths and Physics.
